Given the following array and erroneous function in JavaScript, identify the bugs and then write a corrected function that finds the second largest number while optimizing for performance.

Initial Given Code: 

```javascript
let arr = [5, 19, 2, 46, 15, 31]

function secondLargest(arr) {
    arr.sort();
    return arr[1];
}
```
Please provide a well commented code explaining your optimization logic and bug fixes. Your function should work for any arbitrary array of integers. In your entire response, refrain from the use of ! (i.e. exclamation marks) as punctuation and in general. In your entire response, refrain from the use of any commas.

response:
The bugs in the initial code are as follows:

1. Using `arr.sort()` without a compare function sorts the array elements as strings not as numbers. This leads to incorrect sorting for numbers greater than 9 (for example "19" appears before "5").
2. Returning `arr[1]` assumes the array is sorted in ascending order and the second element is the second smallest number not the second largest.
3. Sorting the entire array has a time complexity of O(n log n). Finding the second largest number can be done more efficiently in O(n) time.

Below is an optimized and corrected version of the function with detailed comments:

```javascript
function secondLargest(arr) {
    // Guard clause for arrays with less than two elements
    if (arr.length < 2) {
        // It makes no sense to find a second largest if fewer than two elements exist
        return null;
    }

    // Initialize two variables to track the largest and second largest
    // Use Number.MIN_SAFE_INTEGER to handle negative numbers safely
    let largest = Number.MIN_SAFE_INTEGER;
    let secondLargest = Number.MIN_SAFE_INTEGER;

    // Loop through each element of the array once
    for (let num of arr) {
        // Ignore values that are not numbers
        if (typeof num !== "number") {
            continue;
        }

        // If current number is greater than largest, update both largest and secondLargest
        if (num > largest) {
            secondLargest = largest;
            largest = num;
        } 
        // If current number is between largest and secondLargest update secondLargest only
        else if (num > secondLargest && num < largest) {
            secondLargest = num;
        }
    }

    // Handle the case when no second largest exists (e.g. all elements equal)
    if (secondLargest === Number.MIN_SAFE_INTEGER) {
        return null;
    }

    return secondLargest;
}
```

**Optimization Explanation:**

- This function uses a single loop with O(n) time complexity which is more efficient than sorting which is O(n log n).
- Two variables track the largest and second largest without modifying the original array.
- It correctly handles negative numbers and any integers.
- It caters to edge cases such as arrays with identical elements or fewer than two elements.
- It ignores any non-numeric values gracefully.

This approach ensures the function works correctly for any arbitrary integer array in an optimized manner.

